\f1\fs22 In the middle of the night, a pair of Elvi barged into Tony\'92s bedroom and rumbled him out of bed. Tony wailed and kicked and thrashed about and it took him until they were marching him down the hall before he regained his wits, such as they were. It didn\'92t help that his fairy boxer shorts were tugged about in the melee; his prouder parts were exposed and were experiencing shrinkage in the night air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What are you doing?\'92 he cried.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Evicting you,\'92 one Elvis answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Evicting me! Why?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You are not the father.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Tony tried to think fast, but all he could do was react. At an opportune point down the hallway, Crasher pushed with all his might into one of his attackers, and wrenched his shoulder forward, lurching the Elvis into a side table, which he knocked over and tripped. Tony crouched, then jumped, loosing the grip of the other Elvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was free. If he could just make as far as the lobby, and could get up the stairs, he could alert Sarah. The Holy Momma would put a stop to this. Wouldn\'92t she? Tony\'92s pounding heart deprived his brain of too much oxygen to process his doubt.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony made it to the lobby, where he was crash-tackled by two GI Blues series who, whether they were more physically capable than the Tenders or no, they believed they were, and that was enough. Tony landed like a plane on an icy runway and skidded the lobby rug into the heavy polished-wood display cabinet by the staircase. Fingers were bent back, ankles were barked. Tony wailed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s going on?\'92 Sarah peered over the bannister at the struggle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sarah! Please tell these goons to lay off.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This one is a fraud, Holy Momma,\'92 a GI Blues said. \'91He\'92s not the father of your child.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So? He\'92s my \'85 friend.\'92 Sarah sounded none too convincing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Friends can live elsewhere, Holy Momma. It\'92s for your protection.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I can protect her,\'92 Tony protested. \'91Ow! Get your knees off my wedding tackle.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah saw Tony\'92s boxer shorts and smiled. She started coming down the stairs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Please stay where you are, Holy Momma.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Indecisive, Sarah stop halfway down. Tony could see her there, wearing an oversized night tee. A trick of the light seemed to cast an orange glow over her belly, and Tony thought he could see a faint outline of a chubby foetus, much older than it could possibly be by that time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony was overcome with a sense of calm. \'91
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Evolve me tender, Evolve me true,\'92 
\f1\i0 he said and blinked in surprise at what he\'92d just uttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The demeanour of the Elvi changed in an instant. \'91What\'92d you just say?\'94 one of them demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony had already forgotten. \'91Ah \'85 er \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92d you just say?\'92 the Elvis asked insistently.\
\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Evolve me tender, Evolve me true,\'92
\f1\i0  one the others repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? Are you messing with us, boy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony was terrified. \'91No, no. Please. I just \'85\'92 He looked up at Sarah and saw the orange glow outline of the foetus again. He blinked against the hallucination, then said, \'91
\f2\i Evolve me at your will.\'92\
